WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:02.502
Narrator: From the cleaning crews

00:00:02.502 --> 00:00:04.537
removing rubber from runways

00:00:04.537 --> 00:00:07.107
and deicers ensuring planes are safe

00:00:07.107 --> 00:00:08.842
for takeoff in the winter

00:00:08.842 --> 00:00:11.211
to the hundreds of dedicated workers

00:00:11.211 --> 00:00:13.313
that deep clean each train car

00:00:13.313 --> 00:00:16.850
for one of the busiest
transit systems in the world,

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:18.785
we took a look at five cleanings

00:00:18.785 --> 00:00:20.620
that happen behind the scenes

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:23.857
in order to keep the travel
industry running smoothly.

00:00:25.458 --> 00:00:26.559
In the wintertime,

00:00:26.559 --> 00:00:29.162
one of the biggest risks airlines face

00:00:29.162 --> 00:00:32.232
is the buildup of ice on
the surface of an aircraft.

00:00:33.099 --> 00:00:36.302
Ice impacts a plane's aerodynamics

00:00:36.302 --> 00:00:38.004
and in extreme cases

00:00:38.004 --> 00:00:42.275
can prevent a plane from creating lift.

00:00:47.047 --> 00:00:48.548
This is Chris.

00:00:48.548 --> 00:00:51.751
He's operating a
specialized deicing vehicle

00:00:51.751 --> 00:00:54.487
that's worth half a million dollars.

00:00:54.487 --> 00:00:58.525
Chris is a certified deicing
instructor for Delta Air Lines,

00:00:58.525 --> 00:01:01.628
and he's been doing
this work for 25 years.

00:01:01.628 --> 00:01:03.930
Chris and his team have one key goal

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:06.699
when temperatures drop below 50 degrees.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:09.102
Chris: We have to have
all the ice and snow

00:01:09.102 --> 00:01:12.205
off the airplane before
we can allow it to go out.

00:01:12.205 --> 00:01:14.607
There's no room for screw-ups.

00:01:14.607 --> 00:01:16.643
Narrator: This is a demanding job.

00:01:16.643 --> 00:01:20.814
Deicers work in freezing
temperatures and extreme weather,

00:01:20.814 --> 00:01:22.949
but that's not the only challenge.

00:01:22.949 --> 00:01:26.019
They're also racing against the clock.

00:01:26.019 --> 00:01:27.921
When flight schedules are packed,

00:01:27.921 --> 00:01:30.990
Chris and his team might
have less than five minutes

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:34.727
to ensure an aircraft is
completely free of ice and snow

00:01:34.727 --> 00:01:36.629
and is safe for takeoff.

00:01:38.198 --> 00:01:40.967
Chris: The small airplanes we
can do in two to five minutes.

00:01:40.967 --> 00:01:42.836
The wide-body airplanes,

00:01:42.836 --> 00:01:44.571
10 minutes to a half an hour,

00:01:44.571 --> 00:01:46.840
just depending on how
much snow is coming down

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:50.877
and how much was on the aircraft
when it was at the gate.

00:01:50.877 --> 00:01:52.579
Narrator: If the time between deicing

00:01:52.579 --> 00:01:55.014
and takeoff exceeds the holdover time,

00:01:55.014 --> 00:01:57.717
after which the deicing
is no longer effective,

00:01:57.717 --> 00:02:01.654
they have to start the entire
process all over again.

00:02:02.589 --> 00:02:04.724
Chris: Sometimes we have
to deice it multiple times,

00:02:04.724 --> 00:02:07.127
especially if there's a runway closure.

00:02:07.127 --> 00:02:08.495
We'll deice the airplane,

00:02:08.495 --> 00:02:11.164
and then they have to
close it for plowing.

00:02:11.164 --> 00:02:12.398
At that point,

00:02:12.398 --> 00:02:15.668
we'd have to re-deice the
airplane all over again,

00:02:15.668 --> 00:02:17.470
and then the runway will be open,

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:19.839
and then they can take off.

00:02:19.839 --> 00:02:22.142
Narrator: Preparations for winter begin

00:02:22.142 --> 00:02:24.077
months before the snow hits the ground.

00:02:24.077 --> 00:02:27.213
Each summer, Delta Air
Lines gathers its 400

00:02:27.213 --> 00:02:30.383
senior deicing agents
from across the country

00:02:30.383 --> 00:02:32.185
for a three-day boot camp.

00:02:32.185 --> 00:02:35.622
They arrive at Minneapolis-St.
Paul International Airport

00:02:35.622 --> 00:02:37.590
between July and September

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:40.627
to hone their skills and learn new ones.

00:02:40.627 --> 00:02:43.830
Then the agents return
to their home airports

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:46.199
to teach others what they've learned.

00:02:46.199 --> 00:02:48.401
In addition to the practical training,

00:02:48.401 --> 00:02:52.238
Delta Air Line deicers must
pass a computer simulation

00:02:52.238 --> 00:02:54.374
and courses in a classroom.

00:02:54.374 --> 00:02:55.975
Chris: One of the reasons
we want to make sure

00:02:55.975 --> 00:02:58.478
that we get all the
contaminants off of the wing

00:02:58.478 --> 00:03:01.347
is so that it doesn't go
through the engine fan blades.

00:03:01.347 --> 00:03:05.418
This is one of our engine
fan blades that was damaged

00:03:05.418 --> 00:03:07.353
from ice coming off of a wing

00:03:07.353 --> 00:03:09.656
and fouling out an engine fan blade.

00:03:09.656 --> 00:03:12.125
It's a very real problem.

00:03:12.125 --> 00:03:15.261
That's why we are all here
deicing the airplanes.

00:03:15.261 --> 00:03:18.331
Narrator: Today, Chris
is on the deicing pad

00:03:18.331 --> 00:03:22.168
leading a practical
training on an Airbus A220.

00:03:22.168 --> 00:03:24.304
He starts with an inspection.

00:03:24.304 --> 00:03:25.838
Chris: When we're doing
our initial inspection

00:03:25.838 --> 00:03:27.807
of the aircraft for deicing purposes,

00:03:27.807 --> 00:03:29.309
we're going to start with the radome

00:03:29.309 --> 00:03:31.911
and see if we picked
up any descending icing

00:03:31.911 --> 00:03:33.179
when the aircraft was landing,

00:03:33.179 --> 00:03:34.781
as it's coming through the clouds.

00:03:34.781 --> 00:03:37.417
We want to make sure that
nothing formed up there.

00:03:37.417 --> 00:03:40.320
We also want to make sure
that before we do any deicing,

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.121
all the avionics bays are closed,

00:03:42.121 --> 00:03:44.524
all the cargo doors are closed,

00:03:44.524 --> 00:03:48.428
and then pay special
attention to our fuel panels

00:03:48.428 --> 00:03:49.596
or the fuel cells

00:03:49.596 --> 00:03:53.066
to see if we've got any
underwing frost forming.

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:55.835
Depending on the wind, of course,

00:03:55.835 --> 00:03:57.370
if we have to shut the engines down

00:03:57.370 --> 00:03:59.606
so we can get in closer, we can do that.

00:03:59.606 --> 00:04:02.308
Obviously safety is always
going to be our number one.

00:04:02.308 --> 00:04:05.478
OK, so we never want to spray
directly into the brakes,

00:04:05.478 --> 00:04:08.147
and we never want to spray directly

00:04:08.147 --> 00:04:09.949
into the suspension of the aircraft

00:04:09.949 --> 00:04:13.019
because we don't want to
wash any of that grease off.

00:04:13.019 --> 00:04:14.487
And if we do, we definitely want

00:04:14.487 --> 00:04:17.557
to notify the crew immediately.

00:04:17.557 --> 00:04:20.059
We definitely want to pay
attention to our nav lights

00:04:20.059 --> 00:04:22.562
because that's where some of
the rime ice is going to form.

00:04:22.562 --> 00:04:24.731
It's a good place to look for it.

00:04:24.731 --> 00:04:28.034
If we find it there, then
it could be on the wing.

00:04:28.034 --> 00:04:29.168
Make sense?

00:04:29.168 --> 00:04:31.337
Perfect, all right.

00:04:31.337 --> 00:04:34.240
Narrator: The deicing process
consists of several steps

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:36.342
and can happen either at the gate

00:04:36.342 --> 00:04:38.978
or at a special deicing pad.

00:04:38.978 --> 00:04:42.615
To start, pilots configure the
wings by lowering the flaps

00:04:42.615 --> 00:04:46.219
and temporarily disabling the
aircraft's ventilation system.

00:04:47.587 --> 00:04:50.189
Chris: So, the deice process is

00:04:50.189 --> 00:04:54.827
very comparable to a car wash and a wax.

00:04:54.827 --> 00:04:56.195
Typically in Minneapolis,

00:04:56.195 --> 00:04:58.464
we'll start at the nose of the aircraft

00:04:58.464 --> 00:05:00.633
and then work our way back,

00:05:00.633 --> 00:05:02.468
spraying the fuselage

00:05:02.468 --> 00:05:04.771
and then the wing and then the tail.

00:05:04.771 --> 00:05:07.573
We usually do at least
a two-truck operation.

00:05:07.573 --> 00:05:08.975
Sometimes if it's snowing real bad,

00:05:08.975 --> 00:05:10.877
we'll have up to four trucks.

00:05:11.978 --> 00:05:13.713
Narrator: The truck can lift the deicer

00:05:13.713 --> 00:05:15.748
30 feet into the air,

00:05:15.748 --> 00:05:18.785
where Chris uses a joystick
to control the nozzle

00:05:18.785 --> 00:05:22.555
that sprays the deicing
fluid at high pressure.

00:05:22.555 --> 00:05:25.858
Each truck has large
tanks that mix the liquids

00:05:25.858 --> 00:05:28.861
to reach specific
chemical-to-water ratios.

00:05:30.063 --> 00:05:31.597
Chris: You like that 2-by-4?

00:05:31.597 --> 00:05:34.500
Half a million dollar
truck, and we use a 2-by-4

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:36.803
to hold these nozzles from spinning.

00:05:36.803 --> 00:05:41.307
So yeah, this is where we
fill the truck with our water,

00:05:41.307 --> 00:05:44.143
our type I, and then our type IV

00:05:44.143 --> 00:05:44.777
anti-icing fluids filled here.

00:05:48.047 --> 00:05:50.917
Narrator: Delta uses two
types of liquid for deicing.

00:05:50.917 --> 00:05:53.720
Chris: We use propylene
glycol, type I fluid,

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:55.254
for deicing the aircraft.

00:05:55.254 --> 00:05:58.091
That's the fluid that we
heat up to 180 degrees.

00:05:58.091 --> 00:06:01.661
And then we'll use
Cryotech Polar Guard Xtend

00:06:01.661 --> 00:06:04.197
for the type IV anti-icing.

00:06:04.197 --> 00:06:07.166
Narrator: On an average
frosty day in Minneapolis,

00:06:07.166 --> 00:06:11.070
deicers can use up to
2,000 gallons of fluid.

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:13.673
On a snowy day, they might need as much as

00:06:13.673 --> 00:06:15.541
50,000 gallons.

00:06:16.642 --> 00:06:19.011
Chris: The blend-to-mix ratio

00:06:19.011 --> 00:06:21.381
depends on the outside air temp.

00:06:21.381 --> 00:06:23.383
So what's actually deicing

00:06:23.383 --> 00:06:28.121
or what's actually melting
the snow or the frost

00:06:28.121 --> 00:06:30.556
is actually the heated water.

00:06:30.556 --> 00:06:33.860
The reason that we add the
chemical to the heated water

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:37.196
is so it doesn't freeze to the airplane.

00:06:37.196 --> 00:06:40.099
Narrator: The last step is
the most crucial of all:

00:06:40.099 --> 00:06:44.871
making sure the airplane
stays deiced until takeoff.

00:06:46.172 --> 00:06:48.207
Chris: The fluid lasts on the aircraft,

00:06:48.207 --> 00:06:51.811
depending on the amount of
frozen precip that's coming down.

00:06:51.811 --> 00:06:54.414
So if there is no precip coming down,

00:06:54.414 --> 00:06:55.948
we just have to spray it off.

00:06:55.948 --> 00:06:57.717
If it's currently snowing,

00:06:57.717 --> 00:07:00.052
then we have to use the type IV.

00:07:00.052 --> 00:07:02.655
So it's kind of like your car antifreeze.

00:07:02.655 --> 00:07:04.857
The more antifreeze you put in,

00:07:04.857 --> 00:07:07.427
the stronger the blend ratio is.

00:07:08.461 --> 00:07:10.963
Narrator: Once a deicer
signs off on the plane,

00:07:10.963 --> 00:07:13.533
the pilot will configure
the flight position,

00:07:13.533 --> 00:07:16.002
taxi to the runway, and take off.

00:07:19.338 --> 00:07:22.875
Chris: We can try and set up
as much trucks and overtime

00:07:22.875 --> 00:07:26.312
and staffing for any given scenario,

00:07:26.312 --> 00:07:29.015
but you never can tell. It's Minnesota.

00:07:29.015 --> 00:07:31.551
Especially when we're
dealing with the weather.

00:07:31.551 --> 00:07:33.286
Narrator: When the boot camp ends,

00:07:33.286 --> 00:07:36.088
all the deicers return
to their home airports

00:07:36.088 --> 00:07:37.423
to share what they've learned

00:07:37.423 --> 00:07:39.792
and get ready for the upcoming winter.

00:07:39.792 --> 00:07:42.128
But for the team here in Minneapolis,

00:07:42.128 --> 00:07:45.231
one of the coldest cities
in the United States,

00:07:45.231 --> 00:07:47.667
this is a year-round operation.

00:07:48.501 --> 00:07:50.269
Chris: The most satisfying
part of the job is

00:07:50.269 --> 00:07:53.005
the last flight of the
night that takes off safely.

00:07:53.005 --> 00:07:56.742
You know, I don't mind
being outside in the winter.

00:07:56.742 --> 00:07:58.244
I'm going to be outside either way.

00:07:58.244 --> 00:07:59.579
Might as well be getting paid.

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:01.647
Narrator: So next time you board a flight

00:08:01.647 --> 00:08:02.982
in the middle of winter

00:08:02.982 --> 00:08:06.152
and see an orange liquid
sliding off the wing,

00:08:06.152 --> 00:08:08.521
you'll know that someone
like Chris is out there

00:08:08.521 --> 00:08:14.961
to make sure your plane is
ready to take off safely.

00:08:21.267 --> 00:08:22.768
[Narrator] Every time a plane lands,

00:08:22.768 --> 00:08:23.970
each tire leaves around

00:08:23.970 --> 00:08:26.539
one and a half pounds
of rubber on the runway.

00:08:26.539 --> 00:08:28.975
Tires are stationary until touchdown,

00:08:28.975 --> 00:08:30.176
when sudden contact with the ground

00:08:30.176 --> 00:08:31.544
causes friction,

00:08:31.544 --> 00:08:32.645
quickly bringing them up to speeds

00:08:32.645 --> 00:08:34.914
of more than 150 miles per hour

00:08:34.914 --> 00:08:35.915
and producing temperatures

00:08:35.915 --> 00:08:38.484
around 500 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:08:38.885 --> 00:08:40.419
That temperature makes
the tread rubber melt

00:08:40.419 --> 00:08:41.687
and bond to the runway

00:08:41.687 --> 00:08:43.990
and lights that guide pilots.

00:08:43.990 --> 00:08:46.826
The Airbus A380 is the
largest passenger plane

00:08:46.826 --> 00:08:49.862
in the world and it has 22 tires.

00:08:49.862 --> 00:08:52.732
That's over 30 pounds of
rubber lost per landing,

00:08:52.732 --> 00:08:54.634
equivalent to the amount of rubber

00:08:54.634 --> 00:08:56.702
in two of your car's tires.

00:08:56.702 --> 00:08:58.804
So how does all of that
rubber get removed,

00:08:58.804 --> 00:09:00.239
and what's so dangerous

00:09:00.239 --> 00:09:02.375
about it building up on runways?

00:09:02.508 --> 00:09:03.809
Mohammed: There is two things

00:09:03.809 --> 00:09:05.111
you need to consider when we're talking

00:09:05.111 --> 00:09:06.946
rubber removal from the runway.

00:09:06.946 --> 00:09:08.948
There's the rubber removal

00:09:08.948 --> 00:09:11.117
from the actual tarmac itself,

00:09:11.117 --> 00:09:12.885
but the other process
is to remove the rubber

00:09:12.885 --> 00:09:14.153
from the lights.

00:09:14.153 --> 00:09:15.721
Narrator: At London's Heathrow Airport,

00:09:15.721 --> 00:09:18.691
where Mohammed works,
an average of 650 planes

00:09:18.691 --> 00:09:21.761
with 10 tires each
touchdown every single day.

00:09:21.761 --> 00:09:24.564
That's 10,000 pounds of rubber daily.

00:09:24.564 --> 00:09:25.598
A buildup in rubber

00:09:25.598 --> 00:09:27.567
can make the surface of a runway smooth,

00:09:27.567 --> 00:09:30.269
decreasing the friction needed to land.

00:09:30.503 --> 00:09:32.505
This can impact braking and control

00:09:32.505 --> 00:09:34.006
and increase the risk of hydroplaning

00:09:34.006 --> 00:09:35.374
in wet conditions.

00:09:35.374 --> 00:09:37.643
And runway lights used to guide takeoff,

00:09:37.643 --> 00:09:40.179
landing, and taxiing, especially at night

00:09:40.179 --> 00:09:42.148
and in low visibility conditions,

00:09:42.148 --> 00:09:45.017
are dimmed by the melted
rubber bonding to them.

00:09:45.318 --> 00:09:46.419
Within the United States,

00:09:46.419 --> 00:09:48.955
the Federal Aviation
Administration recommends

00:09:48.955 --> 00:09:50.623
that rubber deposition be checked weekly

00:09:50.623 --> 00:09:54.293
for any airport with at
least 210 daily landings.

00:09:54.293 --> 00:09:56.062
The process for checking rubber buildup

00:09:56.062 --> 00:09:58.431
isn't as simple as
checking for skid marks,

00:09:58.431 --> 00:10:00.633
and it requires the use of specialized

00:10:00.633 --> 00:10:02.802
tests and instruments.

00:10:02.802 --> 00:10:03.703
Mohammad: So for the runway, we have something

00:10:03.703 --> 00:10:05.271
called the friction test.

00:10:05.271 --> 00:10:07.740
It's like a machine that has a wheel

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:09.442
and someone will drive down the runway

00:10:09.442 --> 00:10:10.910
and that wheel will come down

00:10:10.910 --> 00:10:11.877
and it'll actually measure

00:10:11.877 --> 00:10:15.514
how much friction there is on the runway.

00:10:15.514 --> 00:10:17.650
The key to our runway is you want it

00:10:17.650 --> 00:10:19.185
to have a lot of friction.

00:10:19.185 --> 00:10:21.354
You don't want it to be slippery at all.

00:10:21.354 --> 00:10:23.589
So that measurement of how much friction

00:10:23.589 --> 00:10:26.492
that wheel feels on the
surface of the runway

00:10:26.492 --> 00:10:29.061
will tell our teams when to go out there

00:10:29.061 --> 00:10:30.396
to remove the rubber.

00:10:30.396 --> 00:10:33.032
Now, to do that, we have
specialized machinery,

00:10:33.032 --> 00:10:34.767
which is like a massive truck

00:10:34.767 --> 00:10:36.469
with a Hoover on top of it.

00:10:36.469 --> 00:10:39.005
And so it's high pressure
water, basically,

00:10:39.005 --> 00:10:42.375
but very hot and it blasts the tarmac

00:10:42.375 --> 00:10:43.843
and then it just sucks up

00:10:43.843 --> 00:10:45.711
everything that comes off it.

00:10:45.711 --> 00:10:46.712
Narrator: And then there's removal

00:10:46.712 --> 00:10:48.214
of rubber from the lights.

00:10:48.214 --> 00:10:50.182
Landing technology alone isn't enough

00:10:50.182 --> 00:10:52.852
for pilots to bring planes in safely.

00:10:52.852 --> 00:10:54.620
Mohammad: Of course, they use ILS systems,

00:10:54.620 --> 00:10:56.756
they use the Instrument Landing System,

00:10:56.756 --> 00:10:58.991
when coming into land,

00:10:58.991 --> 00:11:01.527
but they still need a visual reference.

00:11:01.527 --> 00:11:03.229
So from a safety perspective,

00:11:03.229 --> 00:11:04.697
the pilots physically

00:11:04.697 --> 00:11:06.165
won't be able to see those lights.

00:11:06.165 --> 00:11:07.933
And I once spoke to a pilot

00:11:07.933 --> 00:11:11.871
who was flying in from Scandinavia.

00:11:11.871 --> 00:11:13.639
And I remember I always go to the pilots

00:11:13.639 --> 00:11:15.107
and speak to them about
this stuff afterwards

00:11:15.107 --> 00:11:16.776
because I want to get their perspective.

00:11:16.776 --> 00:11:20.012
And I said to him, "What's
it like to fly a plane

00:11:20.012 --> 00:11:22.515
at an airport and land
a plane at an airport

00:11:22.515 --> 00:11:24.950
which doesn't maintain
its lights very well?"

00:11:24.950 --> 00:11:27.853
He said, "It's like trying to fly a plane

00:11:27.853 --> 00:11:29.622
into a black hole."

00:11:29.622 --> 00:11:33.025
So for the lights themselves,

00:11:33.025 --> 00:11:35.795
we do a test where we measure

00:11:35.795 --> 00:11:39.165
how much light output
comes out of the lights.

00:11:39.165 --> 00:11:40.666
So we have this trailer

00:11:40.666 --> 00:11:42.068
that hooks on the back of a car

00:11:42.068 --> 00:11:44.437
and the sensors on the
back of this trailer

00:11:44.437 --> 00:11:46.806
will tell you exactly how much light

00:11:46.806 --> 00:11:49.942
is coming out of every single light.

00:11:49.942 --> 00:11:52.044
And then you get all that
data and you can tell

00:11:52.044 --> 00:11:54.180
which lights have rubber on them.

00:11:54.180 --> 00:11:55.881
So the lights are really important

00:11:55.881 --> 00:11:57.183
and they need to be bright.

00:11:57.183 --> 00:11:59.185
So to do that, we have another machine,

00:11:59.185 --> 00:12:02.088
which uses dry soda and it has somebody

00:12:02.088 --> 00:12:04.056
on the back of the lance and they go

00:12:04.056 --> 00:12:05.257
from one light to another

00:12:05.257 --> 00:12:06.926
and they point this thing at it

00:12:06.926 --> 00:12:10.262
and they shoot powder, this soda powder,

00:12:10.262 --> 00:12:12.231
at the actual lens of the light

00:12:12.231 --> 00:12:13.733
and they get rid of all the rubber

00:12:13.733 --> 00:12:15.668
that is on those lights

00:12:16.602 --> 00:12:17.436
Narrator: From when the last plane

00:12:17.436 --> 00:12:18.270
takes off at night

00:12:18.270 --> 00:12:20.506
to when the first plane
takes off in the morning,

00:12:20.506 --> 00:12:22.274
there's a small window of time.

00:12:22.274 --> 00:12:25.144
Mohammad: Our runways need to
close, so we need to wait

00:12:25.144 --> 00:12:27.747
until the last plane departs,

00:12:27.747 --> 00:12:29.749
and then after the last plane departs

00:12:29.749 --> 00:12:31.050
that's when our teams can come on.

00:12:31.050 --> 00:12:31.917
So if you wanted to clean

00:12:31.917 --> 00:12:34.086
every single light on the runway,

00:12:34.086 --> 00:12:36.222
you can't really do it all in one night,

00:12:36.222 --> 00:12:37.723
because there's that many of them.

00:12:37.723 --> 00:12:39.792
So for the lights themselves,

00:12:39.792 --> 00:12:43.028
we remove it at least twice per week.

00:12:43.028 --> 00:12:44.330
This is something that people

00:12:44.330 --> 00:12:45.464
don't ever really think about

00:12:45.464 --> 00:12:47.600
but there's a whole team of people

00:12:47.600 --> 00:12:50.302
whose responsibility is to make sure

00:12:50.302 --> 00:12:51.804
that these runways are cleared.

00:12:51.804 --> 00:12:54.640
And actually, people benefit from this job

00:12:54.640 --> 00:12:56.208
all the time because the only way

00:12:56.208 --> 00:12:57.710
that they're able to go on holiday

00:12:57.710 --> 00:13:00.679
and enjoy the world is by using

00:13:00.679 --> 00:13:03.883
these strips of tarmac called the runways.

00:13:07.553 --> 00:13:09.522
Narrator: The Tokyo Metro system is famous

00:13:09.522 --> 00:13:12.792
for being one of the cleanest
subway systems in the world.

00:13:12.792 --> 00:13:16.729
It transports an average of
about 6 million people daily,

00:13:16.729 --> 00:13:18.531
and maintaining that reputation

00:13:18.531 --> 00:13:22.535
takes both an extensive amount
of operational organization

00:13:22.535 --> 00:13:24.870
and a ton of manpower.

00:13:28.073 --> 00:13:29.775
Narrator: This is Takayuki,

00:13:29.775 --> 00:13:32.845
a cleaning supervisor at
the rail yard in Ayase.

00:13:32.845 --> 00:13:36.315
He's worked for Tokyo
Metro for almost 41 years,

00:13:36.315 --> 00:13:37.983
and it's his job to make sure

00:13:37.983 --> 00:13:40.219
the trains are cleaned thoroughly.

00:13:52.598 --> 00:13:54.934
The cleaning staff's
approach is straightforward

00:13:54.934 --> 00:13:57.837
but uncompromising: whatever Tokyo Metro's

00:13:57.837 --> 00:14:01.740
millions of daily passengers
touch must be cleaned.

00:14:02.141 --> 00:14:04.109
Today, they're cleaning the train cars

00:14:04.109 --> 00:14:07.847
at the Ayase rail yard,
which they do every 15 days.

00:14:09.014 --> 00:14:11.083
The cleaners take careful measures

00:14:11.083 --> 00:14:13.752
to ensure each nook and cranny is cleaned.

00:14:13.752 --> 00:14:16.655
Then we'll head to the
Ginza-itchome Station,

00:14:16.655 --> 00:14:19.024
located in one of the
busiest shopping areas

00:14:19.024 --> 00:14:20.492
in Tokyo, Japan,

00:14:20.492 --> 00:14:22.928
where they clean the
station top to bottom.

00:14:23.963 --> 00:14:27.666
There are 41 trains on the
Tokyo Metro Chiyoda Line.

00:14:27.666 --> 00:14:30.436
The team cleans three
to four trains a day.

00:14:31.503 --> 00:14:33.572
Once a train comes into the rail yard,

00:14:33.572 --> 00:14:36.275
cleaners start by picking
up any trash they see.

00:14:37.076 --> 00:14:39.879
It takes four workers
to clean the exterior.

00:14:39.879 --> 00:14:42.481
It's all done by hand, and cleaners start

00:14:42.481 --> 00:14:44.683
by hosing it down with water

00:14:44.683 --> 00:14:47.486
and then using brushes
to scrub it with soap.

00:15:24.123 --> 00:15:25.491
Narrator: If the stain does not come off

00:15:25.491 --> 00:15:26.692
with a brush and soap,

00:15:26.692 --> 00:15:28.661
then they use a pad to scrub it off.

00:15:30.362 --> 00:15:33.098
Next, they rinse off all the soap.

00:15:34.333 --> 00:15:37.403
Meanwhile, other staffers
clean the interior.

00:15:37.403 --> 00:15:40.839
It takes 10 workers, and they
start at the top of the car

00:15:40.839 --> 00:15:43.175
and work their way down to the bottom.

00:16:09.501 --> 00:16:11.904
Narrator: The first step
is to clean the area

00:16:11.904 --> 00:16:14.440
where advertisements are placed.

00:16:14.440 --> 00:16:17.910
Then they clean the luggage
racks, and then the wall area.

00:16:17.910 --> 00:16:20.813
They use rags and dusters
to clean each part.

00:16:32.458 --> 00:16:34.093
Narrator: Cleaners then tackle the floors

00:16:34.093 --> 00:16:35.961
and start by mopping.

00:16:35.961 --> 00:16:38.497
They also use sticks to clean crevices

00:16:38.497 --> 00:16:40.332
near the entrance of each door.

00:16:41.900 --> 00:16:45.671
Twice a year they wax the
floors of each train car.

00:16:45.671 --> 00:16:48.207
They remove all the old
wax with a polisher,

00:16:48.207 --> 00:16:50.275
and then they let it dry.

00:16:50.275 --> 00:16:53.912
Once it's dry, they
apply two coats of wax.

00:16:53.912 --> 00:16:55.614
It takes about an hour and a half

00:16:55.614 --> 00:16:57.082
from the time the wax is removed

00:16:57.082 --> 00:16:58.851
to the time the new wax dries.

00:17:16.335 --> 00:17:18.003
Narrator: Altogether, it takes workers

00:17:18.003 --> 00:17:19.138
a little over an hour

00:17:19.138 --> 00:17:21.907
to clean the trains inside and out.

00:17:21.907 --> 00:17:23.609
Once the cleaning is complete,

00:17:23.609 --> 00:17:25.744
Takayuki begins his inspection.

00:18:10.622 --> 00:18:12.591
Narrator: Once Takayuki
approves the cleaning,

00:18:12.591 --> 00:18:14.126
the trains return to service,

00:18:14.126 --> 00:18:18.330
ready to transport millions
of passengers all over again.

00:18:19.965 --> 00:18:22.401
Inside the Ginza-itchome Station,

00:18:22.401 --> 00:18:26.138
Chikako and Kenji work to
clean the station daily.

00:18:49.161 --> 00:18:51.530
Narrator: Kazuhiro has been
working for Tokyo Metro

00:18:51.530 --> 00:18:53.298
since he was 18.

00:18:53.298 --> 00:18:55.234
He got his train license at 20

00:18:55.234 --> 00:18:58.270
and drove trains until he was 57.

00:18:58.270 --> 00:19:01.273
He leads the team responsible
for the cleaning and security

00:19:01.273 --> 00:19:03.609
for this station and a few others.

00:19:03.609 --> 00:19:05.744
There are nine Tokyo Metro train lines

00:19:05.744 --> 00:19:08.413
and 180 stations that provide service

00:19:08.413 --> 00:19:10.382
to the greater Tokyo area.

00:19:39.344 --> 00:19:40.579
Narrator: The escalator handrails

00:19:40.579 --> 00:19:43.081
are a high-touch surface
within the station,

00:19:43.081 --> 00:19:45.751
and Chikako takes extra
care to wipe the belt

00:19:45.751 --> 00:19:47.119
with wet washcloths.

00:19:47.119 --> 00:19:49.254
She rides the escalators up and down

00:19:49.254 --> 00:19:51.123
until they are all clean.

00:20:19.418 --> 00:20:21.019
Narrator: Chikako is also responsible

00:20:21.019 --> 00:20:23.121
for the dirtiest job of the day,

00:20:23.121 --> 00:20:26.558
cleaning the two restrooms
in the Ginza-itchome Station.

00:20:26.558 --> 00:20:28.727
The main cleanings happen once a day.

00:20:28.727 --> 00:20:32.030
She has been doing this
work for the past 10 years.

00:20:58.223 --> 00:21:00.058
Narrator: The first thing Chikako does

00:21:00.058 --> 00:21:02.928
is check for any large pieces of trash.

00:21:02.928 --> 00:21:05.097
If there are none, she sets up a fan

00:21:05.097 --> 00:21:07.866
and leaves it on throughout
the cleaning process.

00:21:07.866 --> 00:21:10.469
Efficiency is one of the
key parts of her job,

00:21:10.469 --> 00:21:12.671
and the fan allows the
bathroom to dry quickly,

00:21:12.671 --> 00:21:16.008
so as not to disrupt any
customers who may need to use it.

00:21:43.201 --> 00:21:45.103
Narrator: Chikako sticks
the ozone-machine hose

00:21:45.103 --> 00:21:47.506
down the toilet and turns it on.

00:21:48.807 --> 00:21:51.243
Then she scrubs each toilet by hand

00:21:51.243 --> 00:21:53.712
to make sure it's clean
for the next customer.

00:21:55.614 --> 00:21:57.516
She also cleans the walls.

00:21:57.516 --> 00:21:59.751
It's important that she's
using the right tools

00:21:59.751 --> 00:22:03.755
for each space, so as not
to cross-contaminate areas.

00:22:03.755 --> 00:22:05.657
She uses a red dust cloth to clean

00:22:05.657 --> 00:22:07.292
areas from the knees down,

00:22:07.292 --> 00:22:09.928
which is called the "contamination zone,"

00:22:09.928 --> 00:22:11.897
and a green one for "clean zones,"

00:22:11.897 --> 00:22:15.734
which are areas above the knees,
such as mirrors and sinks.

00:23:05.917 --> 00:23:08.053
Narrator: Chikako is also
responsible for cleaning

00:23:08.053 --> 00:23:11.857
the barrier between the
platform and the train doors.

00:23:11.857 --> 00:23:13.592
The top part is cleaned daily,

00:23:13.592 --> 00:23:16.595
while the lower part
is cleaned once a week.

00:23:16.595 --> 00:23:19.398
The staff makes sure the
station platforms and walkways

00:23:19.398 --> 00:23:20.732
are clean for customers.

00:23:22.167 --> 00:23:24.569
They do cleanings with brooms every day,

00:23:24.569 --> 00:23:26.671
but they also use a
machine to clean the floors

00:23:26.671 --> 00:23:28.306
twice a month.

00:23:28.306 --> 00:23:31.309
The machine works by sprinkling
water onto the floor,

00:23:31.309 --> 00:23:32.744
scrubbing with a brush,

00:23:32.744 --> 00:23:36.448
and then collecting the
dirty water with a squeegee.

00:23:36.448 --> 00:23:38.884
This way, there is no water left behind

00:23:38.884 --> 00:23:40.652
and no slippery floors.

00:23:47.492 --> 00:23:49.227
Narrator: Kenji also goes around daily

00:23:49.227 --> 00:23:52.097
with a shoulder vacuum
and manually vacuums up

00:23:52.097 --> 00:23:54.199
any trash and dust he comes across,

00:23:54.199 --> 00:23:56.435
especially in hard-to-reach areas.

00:24:15.921 --> 00:24:18.190
Narrator: Japan has a
long history with cleaning

00:24:18.190 --> 00:24:20.992
and cleanliness that persists today.

00:25:15.580 --> 00:25:17.382
Narrator: Now we head off to sea

00:25:17.382 --> 00:25:20.952
and climb aboard one of the
world's largest cruise ships,

00:25:20.952 --> 00:25:23.955
Royal Caribbean's Symphony of the Seas,

00:25:23.955 --> 00:25:27.792
to watch how workers deal with
millions of pounds of water,

00:25:27.792 --> 00:25:31.029
food, and dry waste every year.

00:25:35.500 --> 00:25:38.003
Narrator: This cruise ship is
basically a floating city.

00:25:38.003 --> 00:25:39.638
And just like in a normal city,

00:25:39.638 --> 00:25:42.274
all its residents produce a lot of trash.

00:25:42.274 --> 00:25:43.675
But there aren't any garbage trucks here

00:25:43.675 --> 00:25:45.343
to scoop it up and take it away.

00:25:45.343 --> 00:25:47.345
We're at sea, obviously.

00:25:47.345 --> 00:25:49.347
And since waste can't and shouldn't

00:25:49.347 --> 00:25:51.049
just be dumped in the ocean,

00:25:51.049 --> 00:25:54.185
well, what do cruise
ships do with all of it?

00:25:54.185 --> 00:25:55.453
This is something the industry's

00:25:55.453 --> 00:25:56.821
been dealing with for years.

00:25:56.821 --> 00:25:58.657
Reporter: Carnival Cruise
Line is coming clean

00:25:58.657 --> 00:26:00.358
about polluting oceans.

00:26:00.358 --> 00:26:02.694
Narrator: Princess Cruises
was fined $40 million

00:26:02.694 --> 00:26:04.963
in 2016 for illegal dumping,

00:26:04.963 --> 00:26:08.300
and Carnival got hit with
a $20 million fine in 2019

00:26:08.300 --> 00:26:10.735
for disposing of plastic
waste in the ocean.

00:26:10.735 --> 00:26:12.704
Stewart Chiron: Carnival
Corporation's issues

00:26:12.704 --> 00:26:15.307
really brought the need
for better technology

00:26:15.307 --> 00:26:17.776
so that these ships can
operate more efficiently.

00:26:17.776 --> 00:26:19.511
Narrator: Cruise lines have
been working on systems

00:26:19.511 --> 00:26:22.447
to purify water and deal
with waste inside ships.

00:26:22.447 --> 00:26:23.848
Chiron: Up until now, these types

00:26:23.848 --> 00:26:25.817
of options weren't available.

00:26:25.817 --> 00:26:27.052
Narrator: All this new tech was built

00:26:27.052 --> 00:26:29.588
into Royal Caribbean's
largest and newest ship,

00:26:29.588 --> 00:26:31.389
Symphony of the Seas.

00:26:31.389 --> 00:26:33.491
The company says it's
a zero-landfill ship,

00:26:33.491 --> 00:26:35.827
which means it uses
everything from recycling

00:26:35.827 --> 00:26:38.830
to water filtration to
deal with its own waste.

00:26:38.830 --> 00:26:40.432
And this guy's in charge of making sure

00:26:40.432 --> 00:26:42.601
no single water bottle is unaccounted for.

00:26:42.601 --> 00:26:45.003
Alex Mago: Welcome to
waste and recycling center.

00:26:45.003 --> 00:26:46.204
Narrator: We're down on deck two,

00:26:46.204 --> 00:26:48.340
a secret, crew-only area of the ship.

00:26:48.340 --> 00:26:50.308
Crew members check all
the ship's trash cans

00:26:50.308 --> 00:26:52.611
for recyclables and bring them down here

00:26:52.611 --> 00:26:54.346
for Alex's team to handle.

00:26:54.346 --> 00:26:56.214
Despite being the only waste facility

00:26:56.214 --> 00:26:58.950
on this massive ship,
it's surprisingly quiet.

00:27:00.352 --> 00:27:02.354
Alex said the busiest
time is in the morning,

00:27:02.354 --> 00:27:04.289
when things are unpackaged for the day.

00:27:04.289 --> 00:27:06.725
Mago: This is the waste
streams that we have.

00:27:06.725 --> 00:27:10.428
Every waste stream has its
own way of handling it.

00:27:10.428 --> 00:27:11.830
Narrator: There are
separate teams to deal with

00:27:11.830 --> 00:27:13.465
each incoming recyclable:

00:27:13.465 --> 00:27:16.001
glass, cardboard, plastic, and metal.

00:27:16.001 --> 00:27:17.902
Mago: This is our incinerator room.

00:27:17.902 --> 00:27:20.772
So, we have two incinerators, one and two.

00:27:20.772 --> 00:27:23.541
This area is manned 24 hours a day.

00:27:23.541 --> 00:27:26.978
We have 10 crew members
who are working here,

00:27:26.978 --> 00:27:29.314
five in the morning and
five in the evening.

00:27:29.314 --> 00:27:31.483
Narrator: Crew members
separate glass into colors:

00:27:31.483 --> 00:27:32.984
green, brown, and white.

00:27:32.984 --> 00:27:35.654
[glass clinking]

00:27:35.654 --> 00:27:37.522
Mago: This is the byproduct of it

00:27:37.522 --> 00:27:39.124
after we crush it.

00:27:39.124 --> 00:27:40.358
Narrator: They can process upwards

00:27:40.358 --> 00:27:42.527
of 13,000 pounds of glass

00:27:42.527 --> 00:27:44.095
for a weeklong cruise.

00:27:44.095 --> 00:27:46.331
All the small glass
pieces are stored in bins

00:27:46.331 --> 00:27:47.799
until the ship docks.

00:27:47.799 --> 00:27:50.368
Plastic goes through
this massive compactor.

00:27:50.368 --> 00:27:52.604
Even though the ship's
gotten rid of plastic straws,

00:27:52.604 --> 00:27:54.906
it still relies on bottled water because,

00:27:54.906 --> 00:27:56.307
for health and safety reasons,

00:27:56.307 --> 00:27:58.576
no cruise ship is allowed
to have water fountains.

00:27:58.576 --> 00:28:00.011
So, every week, 
they crush about

00:28:00.011 --> 00:28:02.947
528 gallons of water bottles.

00:28:02.947 --> 00:28:05.550
Mago: We are compacting
the cardboard over there.

00:28:05.550 --> 00:28:07.252
Narrator: Throughout the
day, cardboard is stacked up

00:28:07.252 --> 00:28:09.220
in this machine, called a baler.

00:28:09.220 --> 00:28:12.090
Once it's full, it's all
compressed into bundles.

00:28:12.090 --> 00:28:13.591
And used aluminum cans,

00:28:13.591 --> 00:28:15.727
well, they're sent through this baler.

00:28:15.727 --> 00:28:18.663
The machine squeezes
them down into big cubes,

00:28:18.663 --> 00:28:20.231
which are then stored in a fridge

00:28:20.231 --> 00:28:21.599
just off the waste room.

00:28:21.599 --> 00:28:24.369
Mago: This area is actually for the items

00:28:24.369 --> 00:28:26.538
that can produce smell, the garbage.

00:28:26.538 --> 00:28:28.473
Narrator: And the smell
could get pretty bad.

00:28:28.473 --> 00:28:30.909
The waste is stored for up
to seven days at a time,

00:28:30.909 --> 00:28:32.977
until the ship docks back in Miami,

00:28:32.977 --> 00:28:34.612
where all the plastic, aluminum, paper,

00:28:34.612 --> 00:28:37.615
and glass go to recycling
partner facilities.

00:28:37.615 --> 00:28:39.784
In 2018, Royal 
Caribbean recycled

00:28:39.784 --> 00:28:43.121
43.7 million pounds of waste.

00:28:43.121 --> 00:28:45.490
And any rebates earned from
these recycling programs

00:28:45.490 --> 00:28:47.792
go back to the employee retirement fund.

00:28:47.792 --> 00:28:49.260
The cruise line is hoping that it's a nice

00:28:49.260 --> 00:28:51.429
incentive for employees
to bring recycling down

00:28:51.429 --> 00:28:53.298
from their own crew cabins.

00:28:53.298 --> 00:28:55.934
So, what about things
that can't get recycled?

00:28:55.934 --> 00:28:57.268
For example, food.

00:28:57.268 --> 00:28:58.536
Every week, the ship loads up

00:28:58.536 --> 00:29:00.939
600,000 pounds of provisions.

00:29:00.939 --> 00:29:02.607
But for the food that's not eaten,

00:29:02.607 --> 00:29:03.942
well, the company had to figure out

00:29:03.942 --> 00:29:05.744
how to get rid of all of that, too.

00:29:05.977 --> 00:29:07.278
Each one of the 
ship's restaurants

00:29:07.278 --> 00:29:10.582
and 36 kitchens has 
its own suction drain.

00:29:10.582 --> 00:29:13.651
Chefs and waiters keep food
scraps in separate buckets.

00:29:13.651 --> 00:29:15.053
Then, once they've gotten enough,

00:29:15.053 --> 00:29:17.122
they place it all in this special drain.

00:29:17.122 --> 00:29:19.557
All the food waste ends up in one big pipe

00:29:19.557 --> 00:29:21.693
that runs through the entire ship.

00:29:21.693 --> 00:29:23.194
And that pipe leads to what's known

00:29:23.194 --> 00:29:24.529
as the hydro-processor.

00:29:24.529 --> 00:29:26.297
Mago: Those pipes over there,

00:29:26.297 --> 00:29:27.999
so, this is where

00:29:27.999 --> 00:29:30.468
the food waste is passing through.

00:29:30.468 --> 00:29:32.570
This is being processed through here.

00:29:32.570 --> 00:29:34.272
Narrator: This machine
has a bunch of tiny layers

00:29:34.272 --> 00:29:36.241
of mesh to break down the food.

00:29:36.241 --> 00:29:38.376
Mago: It's being stored in our tank.

00:29:38.376 --> 00:29:41.312
We have two tanks of
comminuted food waste.

00:29:41.312 --> 00:29:42.614
Narrator: And the final step?

00:29:42.614 --> 00:29:44.349
Incineration.

00:29:44.349 --> 00:29:46.951
Now, let's talk about your toilet waste.

00:29:46.951 --> 00:29:48.453
Yep, we're gonna go there.

00:29:48.453 --> 00:29:50.755
It's all a part of the
water-treatment system on board,

00:29:50.755 --> 00:29:52.590
controlled from the engineering room.

00:30:01.733 --> 00:30:04.202
Narrator: Water is divided
into two categories:

00:30:04.202 --> 00:30:07.172
gray water, from sinks,
laundries, and drains,

00:30:07.172 --> 00:30:09.174
and black water.

00:30:09.174 --> 00:30:11.042
That includes everything from the galleys

00:30:11.042 --> 00:30:13.711
and your toilets, including your urine.

00:30:18.650 --> 00:30:20.752
Narrator: The purification
system purifies the water

00:30:20.752 --> 00:30:22.987
to a point above the US federal standard,

00:30:22.987 --> 00:30:24.789
which is almost safe to drink.

00:30:38.870 --> 00:30:40.271
Narrator: Anything that can't be recycled

00:30:40.271 --> 00:30:42.207
or reused on board goes to what's known

00:30:42.207 --> 00:30:43.942
as a waste-to-energy facility.

00:30:43.942 --> 00:30:45.643
Now, we didn't get to
see it for ourselves,

00:30:45.643 --> 00:30:46.878
but Royal Caribbean said

00:30:46.878 --> 00:30:48.913
"heat or gas from the waste is collected

00:30:48.913 --> 00:30:50.281
and converted to energy."

00:30:50.281 --> 00:30:52.717
Chiron: It's definitely
within their best interests

00:30:52.717 --> 00:30:54.385
to be the most environmentally friendly,

00:30:54.385 --> 00:30:57.322
because it significantly can
reduce the waste on board,

00:30:57.322 --> 00:31:00.024
the weight that they have
to carry, the fuel usage,

00:31:00.024 --> 00:31:03.094
and it reduces their
operational expenses as well.

00:31:03.094 --> 00:31:04.562
Narrator: And after one week at sea,

00:31:04.562 --> 00:31:06.164
the recycling gets cleared out,

00:31:06.164 --> 00:31:08.166
incoming provisions are brought on board,

00:31:08.166 --> 00:31:09.500
and the crew prepares the ship

00:31:09.500 --> 00:31:11.803
to start the process all over again.

00:31:14.572 --> 00:31:17.342
Narrator: Every year, tens of thousands of people

00:31:17.342 --> 00:31:20.678
attend Burning Man in
Nevada's Black Rock Desert,

00:31:20.678 --> 00:31:23.081
and many of them rent cars, RVs,

00:31:23.081 --> 00:31:25.984
and trucks to attend the festival.

00:31:25.984 --> 00:31:28.286
But Burning Man can get messy,

00:31:28.286 --> 00:31:29.821
and the desert's playa dust

00:31:29.821 --> 00:31:32.891
that collects on vehicles
is high in alkaline,

00:31:32.891 --> 00:31:36.361
which can corrode a car's metal
and cause long-term damage

00:31:36.361 --> 00:31:37.896
if not thoroughly cleaned.

00:31:38.863 --> 00:31:40.865
Car-rental providers in the area,

00:31:40.865 --> 00:31:42.400
like Enterprise Rent-A-Car,

00:31:42.400 --> 00:31:45.203
brace for Burning Man each year.

00:31:45.203 --> 00:31:47.805
In a typical year, detailers expect

00:31:47.805 --> 00:31:49.474
to find rental cars returned

00:31:49.474 --> 00:31:52.277
coated in layers of dried desert sand.

00:31:52.277 --> 00:31:54.045
But this year's extreme weather

00:31:54.045 --> 00:31:57.382
left vehicles in even
worse condition than usual,

00:31:57.382 --> 00:32:00.251
which meant that detailers
like Miguel and his team

00:32:00.251 --> 00:32:02.186
had their work cut out for them.

00:32:04.289 --> 00:32:06.424
These cars are really, really bad.

00:32:06.424 --> 00:32:08.960
They got covered with
sand, like, everywhere.

00:32:08.960 --> 00:32:10.595
Like, sand everywhere.

00:32:10.595 --> 00:32:13.264
The interior, the outside, the engine.

00:32:13.264 --> 00:32:14.832
This year it rained,

00:32:14.832 --> 00:32:17.368
so this is a different kind of sand now.

00:32:17.368 --> 00:32:21.606
So it's going to be a little
bit harder than usual.

00:32:21.606 --> 00:32:24.575
Narrator: Miguel and his
team must pay close attention

00:32:24.575 --> 00:32:26.077
to the details when cleaning

00:32:26.077 --> 00:32:28.346
the mud-battered rental returns.

00:32:28.346 --> 00:32:30.548
Whether the weather is extreme or not,

00:32:30.548 --> 00:32:33.451
any dust or debris that
settles into the crevices

00:32:33.451 --> 00:32:35.853
of a vehicle has the potential to discolor

00:32:35.853 --> 00:32:38.189
or damage a car if the cleaning process

00:32:38.189 --> 00:32:39.757
is not done properly.

00:32:41.326 --> 00:32:43.294
Today, Miguel and his team

00:32:43.294 --> 00:32:46.130
are focused on detailing
a white Jeep Gladiator

00:32:46.130 --> 00:32:47.665
from start to finish.

00:32:47.665 --> 00:32:49.133
And if they do a thorough job,

00:32:49.133 --> 00:32:53.705
the Jeep will look brand new
for its next group of renters.

00:32:53.705 --> 00:32:56.941
The team begins by assessing
the scale of the job at hand,

00:32:56.941 --> 00:32:59.577
starting with the car's exterior.

00:32:59.577 --> 00:33:01.946
All of the rental returns first go through

00:33:01.946 --> 00:33:05.149
the automatic car wash in
Enterprise's cleaning bays.

00:33:05.149 --> 00:33:06.884
But if a vehicle is returned

00:33:06.884 --> 00:33:08.953
significantly muddier than most,

00:33:08.953 --> 00:33:11.422
it might also need an
initial pressure wash

00:33:11.422 --> 00:33:14.525
to loosen the mud and any other debris.

00:33:14.525 --> 00:33:17.795
After passing through the
automatic wash the first time,

00:33:17.795 --> 00:33:20.298
the muddiest Burning Man cars might need

00:33:20.298 --> 00:33:21.933
a bit more elbow grease.

00:33:21.933 --> 00:33:25.169
For this, Miguel uses a
brush with a long handle

00:33:25.169 --> 00:33:27.305
to loosen any remaining dirt.

00:33:27.305 --> 00:33:29.807
After detailing by hand
with the scrub brush,

00:33:29.807 --> 00:33:32.143
Miguel does another round
of pressure washing,

00:33:32.143 --> 00:33:34.312
then decides if the
Jeep needs an extra run

00:33:34.312 --> 00:33:36.714
through the automatic wash.

00:33:36.714 --> 00:33:39.283
So, right now we just ran
the car through the car wash.

00:33:39.283 --> 00:33:41.119
It looks OK.

00:33:41.119 --> 00:33:44.722
We just gotta wait and see
how it looks after it dries.

00:33:44.722 --> 00:33:46.691
But next, we're going to do the inside

00:33:46.691 --> 00:33:47.558
and we're going to vacuum

00:33:47.558 --> 00:33:50.461
and do the panels and windows.

00:33:50.461 --> 00:33:51.696
Narrator: But before they can start

00:33:51.696 --> 00:33:52.997
cleaning the interior,

00:33:52.997 --> 00:33:55.066
Miguel needs to check under the hood

00:33:55.066 --> 00:33:56.701
to inspect the car's engine,

00:33:56.701 --> 00:33:59.971
which may also have been
coated in fine alkaline sand

00:33:59.971 --> 00:34:01.339
from the Black Rock Desert,

00:34:01.339 --> 00:34:04.042
often referred to as playa dust.

00:34:04.042 --> 00:34:06.077
This stubborn sand is made up

00:34:06.077 --> 00:34:08.379
of remnants from the prehistoric lake bed

00:34:08.379 --> 00:34:10.581
that predated the Black Rock Desert.

00:34:10.581 --> 00:34:13.084
The dust itself is extremely fine,

00:34:13.084 --> 00:34:16.754
which means it can settle into
a vehicle's smallest grooves

00:34:16.754 --> 00:34:19.023
and crevices inside and out.

00:34:20.391 --> 00:34:23.961
The dust's high-alkaline
pH is also corrosive

00:34:23.961 --> 00:34:25.630
and can damage upholstery,

00:34:25.630 --> 00:34:28.299
plastics, and even electronics.

00:34:28.299 --> 00:34:30.535
Burning Man organizers are well aware

00:34:30.535 --> 00:34:33.571
of the playa dust's
potential risk to vehicles,

00:34:33.571 --> 00:34:36.607
as many festivalgoers this
year learned firsthand.

00:34:38.009 --> 00:34:41.012
The official Burning Man
website suggests festivalgoers

00:34:41.012 --> 00:34:43.948
wash their cars immediately
upon returning home

00:34:43.948 --> 00:34:46.217
before the alkaline dust has a chance

00:34:46.217 --> 00:34:47.785
to damage their vehicles.

00:34:47.785 --> 00:34:49.954
It recommends using an air compressor

00:34:49.954 --> 00:34:52.356
to blow the dust off the engine.

00:34:52.356 --> 00:34:54.225
In extremely dirty cases,

00:34:54.225 --> 00:34:56.894
pressure washing may be
the most effective method

00:34:56.894 --> 00:34:59.831
of deep cleaning, but this
requires a level of equipment

00:34:59.831 --> 00:35:02.233
and expertise that's best left

00:35:02.233 --> 00:35:05.269
to trained professionals,
like Miguel and his crew.

00:35:05.269 --> 00:35:07.371
When they're ready to clean the interiors,

00:35:07.371 --> 00:35:09.707
Miguel removes the Jeep's floor mats.

00:35:09.707 --> 00:35:13.010
He scrubs a detergent
deep into the mats' fibers

00:35:13.010 --> 00:35:15.847
and uses the pressure washer
to rinse off the suds.

00:35:17.048 --> 00:35:20.218
Finally, he runs an
extractor over the floor mats

00:35:20.218 --> 00:35:22.620
to remove excess moisture.

00:35:22.620 --> 00:35:27.358
Next, the team gets to work
cleaning every interior surface.

00:35:27.358 --> 00:35:30.128
This phase includes deep
cleaning the upholstery

00:35:30.128 --> 00:35:33.164
and interior doors and
detailing the dashboard,

00:35:33.164 --> 00:35:36.701
center console, gearbox,
and steering wheel.

00:35:36.701 --> 00:35:38.736
They dry-vacuum loose playa sand

00:35:38.736 --> 00:35:40.738
before applying cleaning solutions,

00:35:40.738 --> 00:35:43.441
deodorizers, and disinfectants.

00:35:44.575 --> 00:35:48.112
So, right now, this is with the thing

00:35:48.112 --> 00:35:49.981
that we use for the interior.

00:35:51.215 --> 00:35:54.218
It's a multipurpose solution that we use.

00:35:54.218 --> 00:35:55.319
We're going to use it on the seats,

00:35:55.319 --> 00:35:57.688
hopefully help us with all the stains

00:35:57.688 --> 00:36:00.191
and the sand and the dirt.

00:36:00.191 --> 00:36:02.660
Narrator: They use small
handheld dust brushes

00:36:02.660 --> 00:36:04.428
to loosen ground-in dirt

00:36:04.428 --> 00:36:08.266
and microfiber cloths to wipe
down the hard plastic surfaces

00:36:08.266 --> 00:36:11.435
until they're spotless and dry.

00:36:11.435 --> 00:36:14.505
This sand is different because
it's kind of like chalk.

00:36:14.505 --> 00:36:16.541
It sticks on the panels

00:36:16.541 --> 00:36:18.709
and the car and the tires.

00:36:18.709 --> 00:36:21.479
So it's a different kind of sand.

00:36:21.479 --> 00:36:24.081
That's why we gotta use
these special chemicals

00:36:24.081 --> 00:36:25.550
and the pressure washer.

00:36:26.551 --> 00:36:29.187
Narrator: The last step
of the detailing process

00:36:29.187 --> 00:36:31.455
is drying the floors
and seats of the Jeep.

00:36:31.455 --> 00:36:35.293
Then the Jeep is nearly restored
to its pre-festival state

00:36:35.293 --> 00:36:37.695
and is ready for any finishing touches

00:36:37.695 --> 00:36:39.964
before its big reveal.

00:36:39.964 --> 00:36:41.766
We just finished the interior.

00:36:41.766 --> 00:36:42.967
We did the carpets,

00:36:42.967 --> 00:36:45.536
the seats, and the plastics.

00:36:45.536 --> 00:36:48.206
Right now we're going to do
pressure washing one more time.

00:36:48.206 --> 00:36:49.340
There's still some sand.

00:36:49.340 --> 00:36:51.475
There's dirt under the tires.

00:36:52.610 --> 00:36:56.047
So, we finally finished
with the Burning Man car.

00:36:56.047 --> 00:36:57.415
So if you can follow me,

00:36:57.415 --> 00:36:59.283
I'll show you how it looks now.

00:36:59.283 --> 00:37:03.487
So as you can see, we cleaned
all the mud, all the sand,

00:37:03.487 --> 00:37:05.723
all the dirt in the tires.

00:37:05.723 --> 00:37:08.459
We basically shampooed the carpet,

00:37:08.459 --> 00:37:10.528
the seats,

00:37:10.528 --> 00:37:13.631
and also the driver.

00:37:13.631 --> 00:37:16.867
We also cleaned the
grille, the windshield.

00:37:16.867 --> 00:37:19.403
We got all the bugs.

00:37:19.403 --> 00:37:22.373
This car, as you can
see, looks good as new.

00:37:22.373 --> 00:37:26.344
Took us a couple hours. It's finally done.

00:37:26.344 --> 00:37:27.845
I don't think anybody's going to notice

00:37:27.845 --> 00:37:29.447
that it went to Burning Man.

00:37:29.447 --> 00:37:31.082
That's my opinion.

00:37:31.082 --> 00:37:33.384
I hope the customer agrees with me.

